# Token efficiency (check before executing)

## Before any work
1. Read the active plan’s **current phase / first slice** only. Do not implement later phases unless the user explicitly expands scope.
2. Prefer the plan file + existing code/CSV over re-reading client PDFs (`Agentic_AI_Rockburst.docx`, `sustainability-*.pdf`, `Rockburst_Agentic_AI_Task_Breakdown.pdf`).
3. If the user names a phase (e.g. “Phase 0–1 only”), that overrides the full plan todo list. **Stop when that phase is done.**

## Scope discipline
- One phase (or one named slice) per session unless told otherwise.
- Do not start Phase N+1 after finishing Phase N without an explicit ask.
- End the turn with what shipped and what was deliberately not started.

## Context hygiene
- After Phase 1: use `data/raw/rockburst.csv` (and audit notes), not the supplementary PDF table.
- Do not pull entire large trees into context (`artifacts/`, `.venv/`, model dumps, notebook outputs).
- Do not paste full requirements docs into tools/responses; cite the plan section instead.
- Avoid re-reading unchanged large files; grep/read targeted slices.

## Execution style
- Smallest diff that completes the phase. No speculative agents, ML, or LangGraph before the plan says so.
- Run cheap local checks (`pytest`, short scripts) yourself; only deepen investigation on failures.
- Prefer editing existing files over new abstractions, configs, or docs nobody asked for.

## Stop conditions (mandatory)
Stop and hand back when any of these is true:
- Named phase/slice acceptance criteria are met
- Blocked on API keys, missing data, or a product decision
- Continuing would pull in the next phase’s work
